export const part = "le Queue receipts.\n\nKeep the auto-created 30-minute schedule for Queue pull. The Worker sends one\nselected event in each Queue message, and each sync drains for up to 4 minutes.\nIf doctor reports more than 1,000 messages remaining after syncs, shorten the\ninterval:\n\n```bash\ncnry schedule set <project> --kind traffic-sync \\\n  --source <source-id> --cron \"*/10 * * * *\"\n```\n\nUse these cadences only for Cloud Run, WordPress, and Vercel:\n\n| Cadence | Use case |\n|---|---|\n| `0 */6 * * *` (every 6h) | Production agencies tracking active client sites |\n| `0 0 * * *` (daily) | Lower-traffic sites or local dev |\n| Manual only | First few weeks while validating data |\n\n## Telemetry\n\nEvery successful or failed sync emits a `traffic.synced` event to the\ncanonry telemetry pipeline:\n\n```jsonc\n{\n  \"event\": \"traffic.synced\",\n  \"errorCode\": \"PROVIDER_AUTH\",       // present only when status='failed'\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"status\": \"completed\" | \"failed\",\n    \"sourceType\": \"cloud-run\",        // adapter type\n    \"sourceId\": \"<uuid>\",             // opaque\n    \"pulledEvents\": 234,\n    \"crawlerHits\": 200,\n    \"aiReferralHits\": 12,\n    \"durationMs\": 4150\n  }\n}\n```\n\nCounts are aggregate. The sourceId is an opaque UUID. No raw paths,\ndomains, or PII are surfaced.\n\n## Limits & caveats\n\n- **The WordPress plugin is PHP-only.** The `wordpress` adapter records only\n  requests that reach PHP. Cache-served page requests, including AI crawlers\n  and live AI user-fetches, go uncounted even while crawls of uncached\n  sitemaps, assets, or cache misses appear. On a cached WordPress site, treat\n  this source as a floor unless the selected AI user agents bypass every\n  page-cache and CDN layer. The static doctor warning surfaces this limitation\n  without requesting the origin. Otherwise, use a cache-independent\n  `cloud-run`, `vercel`, or edge-log source. Adapter coverage\n  differs: `vercel` ingests edge request logs so cache hits are captured (it\n  records the `cache` HIT/MISS label), and `cloud-run` logs every request that\n  reaches the service, missing only what a CDN ahead of Cloud Run serves at its\n  own edge.\n- **Path-level citation cross-reference is not implemented yet.** The\n  citation store is domain-grain (`query_snapshots.cited_domains`). A\n  future iteration that lands URL-grain citation evidence will extend\n  the `topCrawledPaths` entry with a `citationState` flag. Until then,\n  treat traffic analytics' crawled-paths table as \"engine attention\" — the\n  signal is the bot fetched it, not whether it was cited.\n- **Verified vs unverified.** Verified crawler counts include only\n  published-IP-range-verified hits. Unverified crawler counts remain separate:\n  those bots claim a known UA but\n  do not match the operator's published IP ranges. The bot can be real or an\n  imitator. Do not promote unverified counts in client-facing copy.\n  **Vercel sources are a special case:** the Vercel pull API returns\n  no client IP, so every Vercel crawler hit is unverified by\n  construction (UA-only). A Vercel source reading 100% unverified is\n  expected, not a misconfiguration.\n- **Four adapters ship: Cloud Run, WordPress, Vercel, and Cloudflare with\n  direct-push and queue-pull delivery. More adapters are planned.** The doctor checks and traffic analytics are\n  adapter-agnostic — adding a new adapter is just a new entry in\n  `traffic_sources.source_type` and a `TrafficSourceValidator`\n  registration.\n\n### Page caches and PHP-only coverage\n\nA page cache (LiteSpeed, WP Rocket, Super Cache, or any\n`advanced-cache.php` drop-in) can serve a request before WordPress boots. The\nplugin therefore has one capture lane only: PHP. It cannot recover a\ncache-served crawler, user fetch, referral, or browser page view after the\nfact.\n\nFor the AI agents measured by this source, configure cache bypasses at every\nlayer that can answer the public request: the WordPress cache, a host/CDN cache,\nand any edge cache. A LiteSpeed exclusion alone is insufficient when an outer\nCDN can return a cached response first.\n\nCache exclusions for the selected AI agents can recover crawler and user-fetch\ncoverage, but they do not recover ordinary browser referrals served from cache.\nUse GA4 for landed browser visits or edge/access logs for cache-independent\nrequest coverage. The `traffic.source.cache-blindspot` doctor check is a static\nwarning; it does not request or impersonate traffic against the public origin,\nand it never sends a `health.degraded` alert.\n\n### Agent request journeys\n\nIn the dashboard, open the project's **Journeys** tab and select **Agent\nrequests**. The sibling **Visitors** view shows browser visitors who arrived\nthrough AI, how many came back, and each visitor's visits in time order. The\nview, period, filters and selected visitor stay in the page URL. Traffic\nsources remains the connection-management page.\n\n`cnry traffic agent-journeys <project>` lists recorded user-triggered agent\nrequest sequences. Open one with `cnry traffic agent-journey <project> <id>`;\nkeep the list's window, source, agent, trust and Property filters. MCP\nequivalents are `canonry_traffic_agent_journeys` and\n`canonry_traffic_agent_journey` in the traffic toolkit.\n\nEach timeline preserves time, hostname, masked path, method, status, available\nlatency, operator verification, trust level with reasons, and current-plan\nProperty attribution. IP/UA grouping is inferred and can combine different tasks\non shared infrastructure; without an IP the requests stay unlinked. Neither\ngrouping nor trust identifies a person or establishes their task.\n\nEvery user-triggered agent request gets a trust level when it is classified.\n`verified` means the source IP is in the operator's published list. Otherwise:\n`likely_fake` when the path asks for credential, config, version-control or\nadmin files, when the same IP asked for one within 10 minutes (`scanner-burst`:\na scanner posing as several agents), when an operator that publishes a\nfull user-agent string (OpenAI, Mistral) sees a different one; an IP missing\nfrom the published list alone leaves a request `unknown`, because lists lag\nreal addresses and some rotate; `likely_real`\nwhen nothing could be checked (no IP, or no published list) but the user agent\nmatches the published string; `unknown` for the rest. `trustReasons` lists the\nevidence. Likely fake requests are never added to AI user fetch hits or stored\nas journey events: hourly totals count them (`totals24h.aiUserFetchLikelyFakeHits`,\n`aiUserFetchLikelyFakeHits` on sync results, `coverage.likelyFakeRequests`),\nand raw samples keep their classification for review. With a Property filter,\n`coverage.likelyFakeRequests` is null: hourly totals carry no page path. When\nthe trust rules change, a server checks stored traffic again once after\nstartup (`cnry backfill traffic-classification` runs the same pass on demand):\nhourly agent rows for a scanner path move to the likely fake counts, and stored\nagent requests from a probing client are removed. Hourly rows keep no IP, so\nolder hours get the burst rule only through stored agent requests.\n\nPreserve the\nresponse's retention, daily-cap and source limitations; missing requests do\nnot prove the agent stopped. HTTP status alone does not prove business success.\nSimple and Advanced projects use the same stored instrument. A Property filter\nuses current-plan URL coverage with its revision; no market, query class, model\nor task intent is inferred. No site snippet or provider call is required to\nread stored events. New recording cannot recover discarded historical samples.\n\n### AI visitor journeys\n\nThe project's **Journeys → Visitors** view uses the same summary, list and\ndetail reads as the CLI. Its period selects visitors by first AI arrival;\nreturn behavior can include later visits beyond that period. Engine and\ncame-back controls filter the list, not the summary. The selected visitor's\nvisits read left to right in time order, and its requests load with\n`events=pages` (pages plus every request that is not GET or HEAD), the same\nfilter as `cnry journeys show --events pages`. These are approximate visitor\nkeys, not unique people, and the scope is project-wide in Simple and Advanced\nportfolios.\n\nEvery sync, backfill and push also follows visitors who arrived from an AI\nengine: their later visits, how they came back (search, direct, social, paid,\nreferral, another AI visit) and each page or API request. It works the same for\nevery source and needs no site snippet or goal setup. Read it with\n`cnry journeys summary|list|show` or the `canonry_journeys_summary`,\n`canonry_journeys_list` and `canonry_journey_get` MCP tools.\n\n- A background call (an API or JSON request) never starts a visit or counts as\n  arriving from an AI engine. It carries its page's referer and utm tags, so the\n  page that made it is the arrival. Calls seen without their page (the page was\n  logged under another key) are not a visitor.\n- A visitor key is an HMAC of IP and user agent under the install secret\n  `journeys.visitorKeySecret` (generated on first `serve` and saved in\n  config.yaml, or `CANONRY_JOURNEY_KEY_SECRET`). No IP is stored. Changing the\n  secret starts new keys.\n- Vercel logs carry no IP, so those visitors are keyed by user agent only.\n  Chrome reports the same user agent for every Mac on one version, so a key can\n  merge people: such visitors undercount people and can overstate return rates.\n  Read `keyBasis` before quoting a return rate. A key stores at most 300\n  requests per UTC day besides AI touches; `cappedDays` counts the days it hit\n  that cap.\n- By default the Cloudflare Worker forwards only AI-referred and bot requests,\n  so journeys from a Cloudflare source record AI landings but not later visits.\n  Reconnect with `--forward-page-views` and redeploy to forward page views too\n  (one Queue message or push per page view; see the Cloudflare setup guide for\n  volume and Queue drain limits). Reads list a source in `partialSources` until\n  its deployed Worker reports the `+page-views` version.\n- Only requests within 30 days after an AI visit are kept, plus the rest of a\n  visit already kept; rows are deleted after 180 days. Journeys start from the\n  first sync after upgrade; backfill a Cloud Run or Vercel source to seed\n  history it still retains.\n- Sign-in, payment and booking-engine return pages (Google accounts, Stripe,\n  PayPal, hosted hotel booking engines and the like) are steps inside a\n  visit, not new arrivals. Listing marketplaces still count as referrals. Query strings keep only\n  `utm_*`; path segments that look like secrets are masked.\n\n### Inspect stored samples\n\n`cnry traffic samples <project> --kind ai-referral --format json` exposes exact\nstored timestamps, normalized paths, status, referer host, user agent and\nclassifier evidence. It never exposes IP hashes. These are a sampled tail with\n30-day retention: pull syncs keep up to 100 per sync, backfills 500 per run, and\ndirect push up to 100 per source per UTC hour. There is no universal hourly cap.\n`totalRows` counts matching samples, not visits. Use `traffic events` for rollup\ntotals; `traffic status` already reports last-24h sample counts per source across\nall kinds. The new read excludes unknown requests by default and requires them\nexplicitly with `--kind unknown`. Viewers and embeds cannot read sample rows.\n\n### Spot new bots and agents\n\n`cnry traffic unrecognized <project>` lists self-identified bots and agents that\nno rule names yet, summed by the name in their user agent (for example\n`hatchfeed` or `example-user`), busiest first, with first and last\nseen hours and an example user agent and path. The default window is the last 30\ndays. People's browsers, generic HTTP tools (curl, python-requests and the\nlike), static files and writes are left out, so the list is agents rather than\nscripts. Every source records them; the Cloudflare Worker forwards them from\nversion 1.1.0. A name that keeps showing up is a candidate for a classifier rule.\nAgents that drive a normal browser (their user agent is plain Chrome) look like\npeople and never appear here.\n\nWhen a new rule recognizes a name, the engine moves its stored hours into that\nbot's crawler or AI user-fetch counts once, shortly after it starts on the new\nrules. Those hits stay claimed, because stored traffic keeps no IP to verify,\nand their path reads `(path not recorded)`, because each hour kept one count\nper name rather than per page. The same pass relabels unknown samples, renames\nrows the naming now reads differently (Apple's link previews become\n`imessage-link-preview`), and deletes Canonry's own monitoring requests.\n`cnry backfill traffic-classification [--project <name>] [--dry-run]` runs it\non demand. An IP-list refresh does not re-verify stored hits.\n"
